  "slug": "resistance-analysing-antifungal-a8e9",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and two published studies test whether learned representations outperform simple baselines across protein, DNA and single-cell tasks.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is whether learned biological representations add predictive value beyond simple baselines. Benchmarks report that DNA foundation model gains over normalized 6-mer baselines are consistent only for NTv3 across 57 classification datasets, and deep perturbation-response models often fail to beat simple baselines across contexts. Layer-wise probing of single-cell foundation models finds intermediate layers can outperform final-layer embeddings. Protein language model work is advancing on several fronts: SCARSE pairs ESM2 embeddings with Gaussian process regression for low-data peptide engineering, SGES exploits a linear fitness subspace for sample-efficient directed evolution, and UPLiFT tunes an activation space using 3-11x fewer trainable parameters. The set is heterogeneous and also includes molecular property modeling, PPI datasets, and CRISPR editing predictors.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-12"
}
